  "output": {
    "answer": "What's in the record is a rule about timing, not strictness: the kill number gets written before I see any output, worded as a single number or condition I can check by reading it — the example given is killing an approach if it loses more than 2.0 points against baseline. E67 shows this in practice: a pre-registered KILL line that fired when the dedup arm lost 14.43 points. But how strict to set that number for a given run — the actual sizing logic — isn't spelled out in what I have here, so I can't say more than that.",
